      "math_academy_id": "e-5815",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242840/assets/84c37b1692f6807873d924fad0d3591298c2b46b780797ed258dcf32d2ff0d8a.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials is shown in the diagram above?\n\nI. $-2 x^4 + 6 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 6 x + 4$\n\nII. $-2 x^5 + 6 x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x + 1$\n\nIII. $3 x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x - 4$\n\nIV. $- x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x - 1$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty.$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\nSo, $P(x)$ is neither II nor III.\n\nLastly, note that the $y$-intercept is $4,$ which means that $P(0)$ must be equal to $4.$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)$ is given by polynomial I.",
